Types pin only that <code>tryAcquire</code> returns a <code>Promise&lt;boolean&gt;</code>. Everything that matters is behaviour-over-time, so it goes in a shared suite: <pre>export function rateLimiterContract(make: () =&gt; RateLimiter, N: number) {
  test('grants at most N per window', async () =&gt; {
    const rl = make();
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; N + 3; i++) results.push(await rl.tryAcquire('k'));
    expect(results.filter(Boolean).length).toBe(N);
  });
  test('a denied call consumes no capacity', async () =&gt; {
    const rl = make(); // exhaust to N, then deny
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) await rl.tryAcquire('k');
    await rl.tryAcquire('k'); // denied
    // after window reset, exactly N succeed again — the deny didn't leak
  });
  test('never grants more than N under concurrency', async () =&gt; {
    const rl = make();
    const r = await Promise.all(Array.from({length: N + 5}, () =&gt; rl.tryAcquire('k')));
    expect(r.filter(Boolean).length).toBe(N);
  });
}
describe('InMemory', () =&gt; rateLimiterContract(() =&gt; new InMemoryLimiter(3), 3));
describe('Redis',    () =&gt; rateLimiterContract(() =&gt; new RedisLimiter(3), 3));</pre> The concurrency test is the one most likely to expose a race in the Redis adapter (non-atomic check-then-increment) that its own tests, run serially, would never trigger. The shared suite makes that drift fail loudly at CI, not silently in production.

